<h3 style=3D"font-family:Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;font-size:17px;col=
or:#12395b;margin:22px 0 8px;">Bottom Line</h3>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">This week again separated biological a=
ctivity from clinically and commercially usable benefit. Del-desiran reduced =
disease-associated RNA in earlier studies but failed its pivotal functional t=
est. Imdelltra, by contrast, extended a proven mechanism into an earlier and =
larger treatment setting, while Isembyld converted a moderate motor benefit i=
nto an entirely new combination layer for SMA.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">The strategic premium is increasingly =
accruing to therapies that do more than engage biology: they must choose defe=
nsible endpoints, fit into existing treatment sequences and offer sufficient =
efficacy or convenience to justify chronic cost and operational burden.</p><p=
